From: SAGA <saga@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 8 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] sched/core: don't fully inherit group's worst burst penalty for top-app forks (BORE)

Incremental on top of "bore" + "bore-topapp" in that order. Context is
the post-bore-topapp state of kernel/sched/core.c.

sched_fork_bore() sets a newly forked task's prev_burst_penalty to
max(own, inherited) -- the WORST burst penalty among its thread group /
siblings / ancestor hub, depending on sched_burst_inherit_type. This
exists so a new task in a CPU-heavy process doesn't get treated as
"fresh" and unfairly win the scheduler for a moment; reasonable
default.

But it also means: when the foreground app spawns a new thread or
process (a very common part of app cold-start and app-switch -- new
render thread, new binder thread, a freshly exec'd process), that new
task can start out already penalized because *some other* thread in
its group happened to be busy a moment ago, even if that busy thread
had nothing to do with what the user is waiting on right now. This is
exactly the inverse problem of what bore-topapp already fixes for the
live burst score -- here it's the *starting* score that's wrong.

Fix: after computing `inherited`, if the forking task lands in the
top-app cgroup, apply the same bounded sched_bore_topapp_discount used
in effective_prio() (reused, not a new knob) to the inherited value
before the max_t(). Units match: sched_bore_topapp_discount is in
prio-index scale (0-39), inherited/prev_burst_penalty is raw burst
penalty (8 fractional bits), hence the "<< 8" to align them -- same
convention as the discount's use in bore-topapp's effective_prio()
patch.

This only ever lowers what the new task inherits; it does not touch
its own subsequently-measured burst behavior, so it cannot mask actual
CPU hogging by the new task itself once it starts running.

Verified: applied cleanly (zero rejects, zero fuzz) on top of a real
android14-6.1-lts + bore + bore-topapp tree.

Signed-off-by: SAGA <saga@users.noreply.github.com>
---
diff --git a/kernel/sched/core.c b/kernel/sched/core.c
+++ b/kernel/sched/core.c
@@ -4533,6 +4533,7 @@
 #ifdef CONFIG_SCHED_BORE
 extern u8 sched_burst_inherit_type;
 extern unsigned int sched_burst_cache_lifetime;
+extern u8 sched_bore_topapp_discount;
 
 #define BORE_BC_TS_BITS   48
 #define BORE_BC_TS_MASK   ((1ULL << BORE_BC_TS_BITS) - 1)
@@ -4725,6 +4726,26 @@
 	}
 	read_unlock(&tasklist_lock);
 
+#ifdef CONFIG_UCLAMP_TASK_GROUP
+	/*
+	 * A freshly forked task in the top-app cgroup shouldn't start
+	 * behind just because a sibling/ancestor happened to be CPU-heavy
+	 * a moment ago -- that's exactly the cold-start case we want fast.
+	 * Same bounded discount as effective_prio(), just applied to the
+	 * inherited estimate instead of the live one (units: raw burst
+	 * penalty, 8 fractional bits -- see calc_burst_penalty()).
+	 */
+	if (sched_bore_topapp_discount) {
+		struct task_group *tg = task_group(p);
+
+		if (tg && tg->latency_sensitive) {
+			u16 discount = (u16)sched_bore_topapp_discount << 8;
+
+			inherited -= min_t(u16, inherited, discount);
+		}
+	}
+#endif
+
 	p->se.prev_burst_penalty = max_t(u16, p->se.prev_burst_penalty, inherited);
 }
 #endif /* CONFIG_SCHED_BORE */
